(registered 2026-11-18, last updated 2026-11-18)

Media type name: application

Media subtype name: vnd.emirian.blueprint

Required parameters: N/A

Optional parameters: N/A

Encoding considerations: binary

The representation is a proprietary binary container with an
authenticated cleartext header and an encrypted body.

The encrypted body contains the workflow blueprint and its internal
approval seal, including the approval statement and signature.
The seal is not a separate file or an unencrypted trailing block.

Blueprint JSON is canonicalized using the JSON Canonicalization
Scheme (JCS, RFC 8785) and compressed using DEFLATE with zlib framing
(RFC 1950) before encryption.

The top-level representation is not JSON, XML, or ZIP.

Security considerations: The format contains declarative agentic workflow definitions, not
embedded native executable code or script programs. However,
interpreting and deploying a blueprint can direct agents,
capabilities, document access, and external operations. Importing or
previewing a file must not authorize or execute its workflow.
Deployment and execution require separate approval, authorization,
and enforcement of tenant capability restrictions.

The blueprint and approval provenance can contain confidential
information requiring confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity
protection. EMRI-1 requires AES-256-GCM encryption of the body, with
the cleartext header included as additional authenticated data.
Each payload-encryption operation uses a freshly generated 256-bit
file key and a 96-bit nonce. File keys must not be reused for a
subsequent payload-encryption operation.

The file key is wrapped using AES-256-KW under the owning tenant's
key-encryption key, following RFC 3394. AES-KW does not require a
wrapping nonce. The wrapped key and its routing metadata are
included in the authenticated header.

Tenant secret keys are maintained server-side within the tenant's
authorized boundary. Unwrapped file keys, tenant key-encryption
keys, and signing private keys must not be included in the file or
embedded in distributed client software. Implementations must
protect key generation, storage, use, backup, and retirement.

Key lookup must be restricted to an independently established,
authorized tenant context. Identifiers supplied by a file must not
authorize access to another tenant's keys. The trusted tenant
context, authenticated header, and signed approval statement must
agree.

Approval is established separately from successful decryption.
An internal seal binds a SHA-256 digest of the canonical blueprint
to its approval context, tenant, blueprint identity, artifact
version, schema identity, approving authority, approval timestamp,
and signing-key identity. The approval statement is signed using
ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256 and deterministic signing under RFC 6979.

The approval statement, content digest, and signature are inside the
encrypted body. Neither a plaintext content digest nor a declared
uncompressed length is exposed in the cleartext header. Header
contents and total container size remain visible.

The verification algorithm is determined by the authorized key
record in the tenant trust store. In-file algorithm identifiers are
cross-checked against that record and must not select an arbitrary
verification algorithm. Unknown or unauthorized keys, mismatched
identifiers, and invalid signatures must not confer approval or
authorize deployment.

Readers must validate container boundaries and lengths and reject
malformed or unsupported representations. Header fields remain
untrusted until authentication succeeds. Plaintext must not be
released for decompression, parsing, rendering, or workflow
processing before successful AES-GCM authentication.

Readers must enforce local limits on input size, decompressed
output, memory consumption, processing time, JSON nesting depth,
and workflow complexity. Output limits must be enforced during
decompression, including for authenticated files. File-supplied
lengths must not override local resource limits.

Compression can disclose information through ciphertext length.
Authoring and export interfaces must not expose an oracle that
allows unauthorized parties to combine attacker-controlled input
with undisclosed blueprint content and repeatedly observe resulting
file sizes. Compressing each blueprint separately does not remove
all content-dependent size leakage.

The JSON security considerations in RFC 8259, Section 12, and the
JCS security considerations in RFC 8785, Section 5, apply to JSON
structures in the format. Readers must parse JSON as data, never
evaluate it as code, reject duplicate object member names, and
enforce the applicable schema, canonicalization rules, numeric
constraints, and workflow semantic checks before use.

Blueprints can reference agents, capabilities, documents, and other
resources outside the file. Resolution of these references requires
tenant authorization. Their presence in an authenticated or signed
file does not independently grant access or authorize automatic
external retrieval.

A valid seal does not establish that approval remains current or
that the artifact is the latest authorized version. Deployment must
apply current tenant policies for approval status, key trust,
revocation, artifact versions, and replay or rollback prevention.

Encryption maintenance must preserve the original content approval
only when the signed content and approval statement are unchanged.
Changing authenticated container metadata requires a newly
authenticated container. Re-encryption must use a fresh file key
and nonce; it must not reuse the original key and nonce with changed
authenticated data. Maintenance must not be represented as a new
human approval.

The format does not prevent an authorized recipient from disclosing
decrypted content and does not protect plaintext in a compromised
authorized processing environment.

References:
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3394.html
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5116.html
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6979.html
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259.html#section-12
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8785.html#section-5
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8725.html#section-2.4

Interoperability considerations: Compatibility depends on support for the container version,
blueprint schema, and cryptographic profile used by the file.
Readers must reject unsupported container versions, incompatible
schemas, and unsupported cryptographic profiles rather than
guessing or silently substituting alternatives.

Published specification: N/A

Status: in preparation; not yet published.

Proprietary format controlled by Archiplexus Architects LLC.

Contact for specification and implementation inquiries: formats&emirian.ai

Applications which use this media: Emirian Agentic Governance Platform, developed by Archiplexus
Architects LLC.

Emirian Mandate provides visual authoring of governed multi-agent
workflow blueprints. Emirian Global Command provides blueprint
governance, approval, and controlled deployment.

The media type is intended for blueprint interchange and archival
storage within the owning tenant's authorized environment.

Fragment identifier considerations: Fragment identifiers are not defined for this media type.

Restrictions on usage: This proprietary format is intended for creation and interpretation
by licensed installations of the Emirian Agentic Governance
Platform.

Additional information:

1. Deprecated alias names for this type: N/A
2. Magic number(s): 45 4D 52 49 1A 0A (hexadecimal; 6 octets at byte offset 0).
3. File extension(s): .emri, .emir
4. Macintosh file type code: N/A
5. Object Identifiers: N/A

General Comments: A proprietary, sealed, tenant-bound governed workflow definition
authored in the Emirian Agentic Governance Platform, with associated
provenance and approval metadata.

The protected workflow content is cryptographically bound to the
owning licensed tenant and carries an approval signature issued by
that tenant's governance authority. Access to the protected content
requires tenant-authorized decryption. Deployment additionally
requires valid tenant approval and applicable permissions.

General-purpose tools can store or transfer the container but cannot
directly interpret its encrypted workflow content.

Person to contact for further information:

1. Name: Archiplexus Architects LLC
2. Email: formats&emirian.ai

Intended usage: LIMITED USE

Intended for governed workflow blueprint interchange and archival
storage within licensed Emirian Agentic Governance Platform
environments.

General-purpose interpretation or deployment outside the platform
is not supported.

